A||||6||Kelvin Vivian||Protecting Against Paint Fade||||5||
Z||000000||Kelvin Vivian||02-28-2001||11:06 PM||||I wash my cars, I wax my cars. There's nothing finer than a clean Fiero cruising down the road.<P>However, slowly, over time, the original paint starts to oxidize. Is there a way to protect against oxidization? It seems like wax dries the paint out and quickens the oxidation process. Will an application of clear coat prevent oxidization?<P>Kelvin<BR>87 SE 4-cyl, med metallic red/grey, 5-spd<BR>88 GT T-top, bright red/tan, 5-spd<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/no_18/finalfront.jpg">    1987 SE - 4-cyl, 5-spd||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||02-28-2001||11:31 PM||||Nothing beats a good car cover.  You can wax and wax and wax, but the sun will eventully win......the end result is your Fiero fades. <P>I have (reluctantly) stored my 16K mile '88 GT outside for the past 3 years.  I have a series of covers on it (three total) and it looks as good now as when I bought it.  You have to be religious about putting the cover on, which can be a pain sometimes.  <P>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||gabriel||03-01-2001||12:07 AM||||Or you could drive it only on cloudy days & at night ...    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> hehe.<P>  Down here the sun does a job on paint pretty quickly.  I recommend the cover. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||03-01-2001||12:10 AM||||My car sat outside for 9 of it's 15 years.<BR>It still shines like new. I only use Meguires wax and polishes. Every spring my car gets the Deep Crystal 3-step cleaner/polish/wax treatment. Just waxing will dry out the paint. The cleaners and polishes put moisture back into the paint to aid in reducing fade. (Also the color of the car matters too. Some colors, mostly reds and blues, fade faster than others so you need to polish it more often.) ||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||03-01-2001||12:52 AM||||It also depends on where you live.  Here in Denver the suns rays are more intense than at lower elevations, so that's something to consider.<P>Another consideration is your interior.  My interior still looks like it did the day it rolled off the assembly line.  True, it only has 16K miles (and thus, should look new), but if it sat uncovered for 3 years the speaker covers/dash would have faded by now.  <P><BR>I think a combination of good wax/polish (like Oreif suggested) and cover will protect your Fiero for many years to come.<p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 03-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tigger||03-02-2001||11:59 AM||||Get a good car cover, don't go cheap.  The el-cheap-o's can be abrasive to the paint, especially if the cover is blowing in the wind.  This is why I don't put a cover on my car when it's outside and especially if the car is dirty.  Just try to park in shady spots whenever possible, next to a building etc, but not under trees.||||reg||1
Z||000006||b lo 0||03-02-2001||03:12 PM||||There's not a whole lot you can do besides the car cover, but when it fades, sand the whole car with 1500-2000 grit wetordry paper (wet sand it) and machine compuond it (buff & polish).  Time consuming and tiring, but WELL worth it... trust me on this, I am a collision repair technician and painter (by schooling, not trade).   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
